messages.content:
A tiny bakery website

Screens of this mockup, as a tree:
@home: the first screen of A tiny bakery website (this screen)
  @product-details: Details screen for the sourdough loaf of the day, price 7.50 USD, in stock
  @order-confirmation: The order confirmation screen
  @croissant-details: Details screen for the butter croissant, price 3.20 USD, in stock
  @menu: The menu screen of A tiny bakery website (not made yet)
  @order-checkout: The order checkout screen, opened by Cart, with 1 sourdough loaf in the cart (not made yet)

The spec of this mockup: decisions every screen follows, one per line; a row with ? is not decided yet.
product.core-flow = @home → @menu → @order-checkout → @order-confirmation
ui.nav = Tabs: Home @home, Menu @menu, Cart @order-checkout
ui.back = ‹ Back, first in the header
ui.forms = Labels above inputs, full-width inputs, primary button full width in dark brown
ui.lists = Horizontal cards: photo banner, name, price with availability dot
ui.colors = Primary action dark brown; unavailable grey; error red; success green
data.prices = Prices in USD with two decimals, tax included
data.fulfillment = Orders show pickup time and place, no delivery address

Tabs of ui.nav: last on every screen, in this markup, one link per tab with its label and @name, and aria-current="page" on this screen's own tab: <nav data-ui="tabs"><a data-action="@name">Label</a>…</nav>. A ui.nav line names each tab by its label and @name, e.g. ui.nav = Tabs: Menu @home, Orders @order-list, Profile @profile
Back of ui.back: where ui.back says, with its label, in this markup: <a data-ui="back" data-action="back">Label</a>. The tab screens have no Back. A ui.back line starts with the label, e.g. ui.back = ‹ Back, first in the header

Design it for a phone screen, 390 px wide.

